---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactButtons from '../../components/ContactButtons.astro';
import { getTranslation } from '../../i18n/utils';
import type { Locale } from '../../i18n/languages';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'zh' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'tr' } },
    { params: { lang: 'ar' } },
    { params: { lang: 'ro' } },
    { params: { lang: 'he' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const locale = lang as Locale;
const t = getTranslation(locale);
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  title={t.seo.whyTitle}
  description={t.seo.whyDesc}
>
  <article class="py-16 sm:py-20 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

      <header class="text-center mb-12">
        <span class="inline-block px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 bg-brand-50 dark:bg-slate-800 rounded-full mb-4">
          Evidence-Based Approach
        </span>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-white leading-tight">
          Why Speaking with a Native Speaker Outperforms Textbooks Alone
        </h1>
      </header>

      <div class="prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-8 text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- Answer-first intro block -->
        <div class="p-6 bg-brand-50/50 dark:bg-slate-800/80 rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 dark:border-slate-700">
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white m-0">
            <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Live conversation forces active retrieval, trains speech muscle memory, and provides contextual pronunciation feedback in real-time. Textbooks build recognition memory, but live speaking builds spontaneous communication confidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">1. Real-Time Retrieval Practice vs. Passive Recognition</h2>
          <p>
            When reading a textbook or filling out grammar exercises, your brain operates in <em>recognition mode</em>. You recognize the correct answer because it is presented right in front of you. However, when speaking in a live conversation, your brain must perform <em>active retrieval</em>: searching your memory for the right word, applying case endings, and forming sounds—all within milliseconds. Cognitive psychology demonstrates that active retrieval strengthens neural pathways far more effectively than passive reading.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">2. Immediate Pronunciation & Word Stress Feedback</h2>
          <p>
            Russian is notorious for shifting word stress. For example, the word <strong>замок</strong> means <em>castle</em> when stressed on the first syllable (зáмок), but <em>lock</em> when stressed on the second syllable (замóк). Language apps cannot listen to your sentence cadence or correct subtle intonation errors. A native speaker corrects these nuances instantly during live dialogue, preventing bad habits from becoming permanent.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">3. Modern Everyday Russian vs. Archaic Textbook Phrases</h2>
          <p>
            Textbooks often teach overly formal or dated expressions that sound unnatural in contemporary conversation. Live sessions with Vasily Moish expose you to authentic, modern Russian as spoken today, including natural filler words, conversational transitions, and current colloquialisms.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">4. Overcoming the Freeze Response and Building Confidence</h2>
          <p>
            The biggest barrier for intermediate language learners is fear of making mistakes in front of others. By practicing 1-on-1 with a patient, encouraging native speaker, you create a safe environment where errors are treated as valuable learning opportunities rather than failures. Over time, your anxiety decreases and spontaneous speech becomes second nature.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">5. Cultural Context and Idiomatic Expressions</h2>
          <p>
            Language is deeply intertwined with culture. Live conversations provide rich context behind common Russian metaphors, humor, and cultural references that cannot be captured in grammar tables.
          </p>
        </section>

      </div>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="mt-16 pt-12 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">
        <h3 class="text-center text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-6">
          Ready to experience the difference live practice makes?
        </h3>
        <ContactButtons locale={locale} showQRCodes={false} />
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
